  "transcript": "# Regulatory sandboxes in financial services\n\n## What a regulatory sandbox is\n> A sandbox is a supervised, limited test space for new financial products and business models; it serves both firms and regulators but takes many forms.\n\nChair: Let's start: briefly, what is a regulatory sandbox and what is it for?\nProfessor Markos Zachariadis: A sandbox is a controlled environment where firms—often fintechs but also incumbents—test new products, services or business models with real or simulated users under regulator supervision.\nProfessor Markos Zachariadis: They act as learning devices for regulators and firms, and as regulatory devices to help firms meet rules while experimenting.\nProfessor Hilary Allen: Jurisdictions vary. Sandboxes also signal a country is open to fintech. Their stated goals are regulatory learning and lowering entry barriers for fintechs.\n\n\n## Who benefits and what evidence exists\n> Sandboxes help participating firms raise finance and test ideas, but there is limited evidence they deliver public benefit or long-term consumer gains.\n\nLord Sharkey: How do operators judge success? Who benefits, and is there a counterfactual to show real public benefit?\nProfessor Hilary Allen: Most impact studies look at firm-level metrics—funding and patents. Regulators’ early reports focus on innovators' experiences but not public welfare.\nProfessor Markos Zachariadis: Research finds sandbox firms gain better access to finance—partly a signalling effect—but there is sample bias and weak evidence on consumer welfare.\nLord Sharkey: Why aren't there agreed metrics?\nProfessor Markos Zachariadis: There are possible metrics—competition, consumer outcomes—but regulators hold much of the data. Independent researchers need more safe data access.\nLord Vaux of Harrowden: If evidence is weak, why have sandboxes spread worldwide?\nProfessor Hilary Allen: They spread because they were an easy signalling tool: say you welcome fintech. But running them properly is costly, so some countries later regret it.\nProfessor Markos Zachariadis: Also, they can help regulators learn and reduce future compliance costs by clarifying rules—if run well—but that claim still needs better empirical backing.\n\n\n## AI, durability and regulatory risks\n> Sandboxes can be risky for AI because AI's data links and ongoing learning don't fit short-term, bespoke testing; regulators may end up allowing permanent exceptions.\n\nLord Hollick: Can sandboxes help regulate AI-driven services that guide consumers?\nProfessor Hilary Allen: Sandboxes are temporary. At the end, regulators face a choice: extend exemptions or shut firms down. In Canada many crypto firms stayed on exempt status—problematic.\nProfessor Hilary Allen: Sandboxes risk becoming bespoke negotiations between a firm and regulator, excluding public input, and can encourage firms to avoid full compliance.\nProfessor Markos Zachariadis: AI is different: it draws on open data and adapts. Sandboxes should have safeguards or different methods for AI testing—sandbox conditions may not mirror live systems.\n\n\n## Alternatives and improvements\n> Regulators can use innovation hubs, guidance, thematic sandboxes, broad waivers and targeted challenges instead of—or alongside—traditional sandboxes.\n\nLord Hill of Oareford: If sandboxes aren't ideal, what alternatives exist to encourage regulatory innovation?\nProfessor Hilary Allen: Innovation hubs (or 'concierge' guidance) offer bespoke guidance without bespoke legal exceptions. If laws truly need change, issue broad waivers rather than favouring chosen firms.\nProfessor Markos Zachariadis: Other tools include digital sandboxes with synthetic data, regulatory tech sprints and open challenges—these can produce learning without permanent legal carve-outs.\nLord Turnbull: Are there clear success stories—firms that used a sandbox and became household names?\nProfessor Markos Zachariadis: Some well-known fintechs engaged with the FCA sandbox era, but attributing their success solely to sandbox participation is hard; causation hasn't been proved.\nProfessor Hilary Allen: In the US there was no big sandbox movement, yet fintech funding flourished—so sandboxes aren't the only path to industry growth.\n\n\n## Transparency, consumer focus and governance\n> More transparency, independent evaluation, published criteria for admission, and consumer-focused metrics are needed to avoid bias and hidden deregulation.\n\nBaroness Bowles of Berkhamsted: I'm worried about opacity, hidden law modification, and embedded model preferences. How to respond?\nProfessor Markos Zachariadis: Regulators should publish more systematic lessons, anonymised data for researchers, and clearer reporting on consumer outcomes—while protecting sensitive data.\nProfessor Hilary Allen: Sandboxes sit in an environment that reflexively prioritises innovation. That can peel back laws designed to protect the public. Public and parliamentary scrutiny should be stronger.\nLord Lilley: Regulators need to learn how law applies to new tech—but that doesn't mean repealing rules. How far should guidance go?\nProfessor Hilary Allen: Guidance helps apply existing laws to new tech. Law is often flexible; regulators should avoid reflexively removing rules instead of finding workable applications.\nLord Davies of Brixton: Can sandboxes be recast to serve consumers rather than providers?\nProfessor Hilary Allen: In principle yes, but sandboxes naturally build close ties with providers, raising cognitive capture risks. Measuring consumer benefit is hard and underdone.\nBaroness Donaghy: What if the regulator is just promoting a fashionable idea? How to call out the 'emperor's new clothes'?\nProfessor Hilary Allen: It's often not malice but groupthink and fear of missing out. Independent scrutiny, willingness to question orthodoxy, and rigorous evidence standards help expose weak claims.\nProfessor Markos Zachariadis: Regulators will share data and discuss improvements if asked. More research, safely shared data and targeted experiments—plus clearer goals—would strengthen accountability.\n",
  "inquiry": "Regulatory sandboxes in financial services",
